  There's no way to get unlimited spell casting.  Even an XL 30
Wizard with Int 23, Wis 23, the Eye and 1000+ max mana points
can easily run out of Mana.  (Ask me how I know.  :-)

  So all characters who rely on spells will have to limit
spell casting due to finite mana.


  As for writing a spellbook, wizards with maximum luck have
a 98% chance of writing an unknown spellbook.  Other classes
top out at about 33%, as I recall.


  Polying for unknown spellbooks burns up a lot of spellbook
reads, as each time a spellbook is poly'd its "read counter"
is incremented.  (There are similar effects with chargeable 
objects.)

  Frequently the "new" spellbooks resulting from polymorphing
have a "read-counter" so high that they are only useful for
learning new spells.  (You can still learn a completely unknown 
spell from a spellbook with a counter greater than 2.)


  20,000 turns seems like a lot.  Most of that time will be
spent traveling, however, or doing other things.  (Speed
ascenders probably don't worry about this.  Speed ascenders
have bigger problems.)


  Granted, once you know the spell then writing a new spellbook 
is guaranteed.  And you can learn a lot of new spells that way.
But you'll rarely get enough marker charges to replace all the 
spellbook reads you've lost.


  And, as has just been pointed out, your use of the spell will 
be limited by the mana you generate during that time.  You'll 
probably want to use some mana to cast other spells as well.  

  (Knowing a lot of spells at once is like serving multiple 
prison sentences concurrently.  You're burning up the time as 
quickly as possible.  When you're in prison that's a good thing.
With spell knowledge it's not so good.)


  So be careful what spellbooks you poly.  Use spares, and
of useless spells at that, bless 'em.  (Few spells are 
completely useless, given the availability of robes and HoB.)



